					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "The Liquefier"
			x: 0.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"6E06FDD802380DCB"
			]
			description: [
				"Liquid air is cryogenic. In steel or iron pipes, pumps or tanks, or in PVC, it frosts them white, then they crack and burst. Copper, aluminium and glass take it, and tooltips say which crack."
				""
				"Spilt, it boils away in seconds, freezing the water beside it."
			]
			id: "5CDB9FE65D68D6E3"
			rewards: [ ]
			subtitle: "Cold enough to crack steel"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "0C9EA2B4F9F6B25F"
					item: "mcremix:liquid_air_bucket"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Liquid Air"
			x: 1.75d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"5CDB9FE65D68D6E3"
			]
			description: [
				"Aluminium Column Sections join as steel ones do, but need no heat and no condenser: liquid air boils in the world's warmth. It splits into nitrogen from the top, oxygen from the bottom and argon from a section of its own between them. Spare sections sit just under the top and draw nothing, and goggles show which section draws what."
				""
				"Argon and oxygen boil only 3 °C apart, so the column needs extra sections between them, or liquid nitrogen piped back into its top as reflux. It frosts and grows icicles as it runs, and melts beside anything &6Hot&r."
			]
			id: "4A92C81CCC100231"
			rewards: [ ]
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.5d
			subtitle: "Boiled by the world's warmth"
			tasks: [
				{
					icon: "mcremix:aluminium_column_section"
					id: "6D7B9EB28CE4A5E8"
					observe_type: 2
					timer: 10L
					title: "Look at an air column grown icicles"
					to_observe: "mcremix:aluminium_column_section[frost=2]"
					type: "observation"
				}
			]
			title: "The Air Column"
			x: 3.5d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"4A92C81CCC100231"
			]
			description: [
				"Piped into a lit Steel Furnace beside its fuel, oxygen makes the fire burn twice as hot and lifts the fuel's ceiling a level. Let loose, it makes fire spread."
			]
